WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.540
When a data engineer sets up

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.260
these bindings, what kind of systems

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.880
are they actually reaching into?

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.540
According to the source documentation,

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.860
the bindings and Fabric ontologies

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.720
can point to a surprisingly wide

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.059
variety of data architectures.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.800
Like what? You can bind to

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.780
lake houses, which are typically used

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.540
to store massive amounts of operational

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.620
reference data, historical inventory

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.840
records, and shipment logs in open

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.960
formats. OK. You can bind to

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.960
event houses or KQL databases, which

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.780
are specifically architected to handle

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.220
high volume real -time streaming telemetry.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.559
Streaming like IoT devices.

00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:35.980
Exactly, like the second -by -second

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.260
temperature readings from thousands

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.620
of refrigeration units.

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:40.500
You can point to traditional relational

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.279
data warehouses, standard SQL databases,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.100
externally mirror databases, and as

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.300
we discussed, power BI semantic models.
